<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">26 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 26 March, from 590 to 1995.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">590 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,436 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Maurice proclaims his son Theodosius co-emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Eastern Roman emperor Maurice made his eldest son Theodosius, Caesar since 587, his co-emperor. Theodosius was deposed in a military revolt and was said to have been executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a>'s supporters.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1244">1244 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">782 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">James I of Aragon signs the Treaty of Almizra with Castile</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-almizra">Treaty of Almizra</a> with the Castilian heir Alfonso, fixing the frontier between their conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> went to Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, setting the southern limit of Aragonese expansion.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-almizra">Treaty of Almizra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/combat-of-the-thirty" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/combat-of-the-thirty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1351">1351 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">675 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Franco-Breton side wins the Combat of the Thirty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-breton-succession">War of the Breton Succession</a>, thirty champions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a> under Jean de Beaumanoir met thirty men of the Anglo-Breton garrison of Ploërmel under Robert Bemborough. The Franco-Breton side won, and the fight became a celebrated example of chivalry.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/combat-of-the-thirty">Combat of the Thirty</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1484">1484 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">542 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">William Caxton prints his English translation of Aesop's Fables</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s first printer completed his edition of the 'Fables of Esope' at Westminster, illustrated with woodcuts. Caxton's books helped standardise written English.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1812">1812 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">214 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Earthquake devastates Caracas and Venezuela</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On Holy Thursday a powerful earthquake destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and other towns held by the young republic, killing thousands. Royalist clergy called it divine punishment, and the First Republic soon fell.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1827">1827 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">199 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ludwig van Beethoven dies in Vienna</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Beethoven died aged 56 during a thunderstorm. Despite growing deafness he composed nine symphonies and late string quartets that changed Western music, and some 20,000 people attended his funeral.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1971">1971 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">55 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bangladesh declares independence from Pakistan</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After the Pakistani army's crackdown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, independence was proclaimed in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a>. Nine months of war followed, and Bangladesh won its freedom in December 1971.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1979">1979 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">47 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Egypt and Israel sign a peace treaty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At the White House, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> and Menachem Begin signed the first peace treaty between Israel and an Arab state, building on the Camp <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Accords. Israel returned the Sinai by 1982.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1995">1995 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">31 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Schengen Agreement abolishes border checks in Europe</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Seven European countries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, removed passport controls at their common borders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> now covers most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and several other states.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 26 March <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-massey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-massey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William Massey</span><span class="tp">1856–1925</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/fuad-i-of-egypt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fuad-i-of-egypt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Fuad I of Egypt</span><span class="tp">1868–1936</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/syngman-rhee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Syngman Rhee</span><span class="tp">1875–1965</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-prince-of-albania"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-prince-of-albania.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William, Prince of Albania</span><span class="tp">1876–1945</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 26 March <span class="n">8</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ludger"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ludger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ludger</span><span class="tp">c. 742–809</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/adud-al-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Adud al-Dawla</span><span class="tp">936–983</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sigurd-the-crusader.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Sigurd the Crusader</span><span class="tp">c. 1090–1130</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alfonso-xi-of-castile.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alfonso XI of Castile</span><span class="tp">1311–1350</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-winthrop.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">John Winthrop</span><span class="tp">1588–1649</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ludwig-van-beethoven.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ludwig van Beethoven</span><span class="tp">1770–1827</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/barghash-bin-said"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/barghash-bin-said.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Barghash bin Said</span><span class="tp">c. 1837–1888</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cecil-rhodes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Cecil Rhodes</span><span class="tp">1853–1902</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
